<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the MAC APP STORE build only.

  Referenced from tauri.appstore.conf.json, never from tauri.conf.json — the
  Developer ID / DMG build deliberately ships UNSANDBOXED, because sandboxing it
  would buy nothing and could only break file access. Do not move these into the
  base config.

  Paintlet needs a small set of entitlements: no printing, no camera or
  microphone, no location, and no recents list. It reads and writes exactly the
  files the user picks in a native panel, and that is all.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- Mandatory for the App Store. Confines the app to its own container. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      Read/write access to files the user explicitly chooses in an open or save
      panel. This is what makes src/lib.rs's read_image_file / write_image_file
      keep working under the sandbox: the panel (Powerbox) is a trusted system
      process, and picking a file hands this process a sandbox extension for
      that exact path. std::fs then inherits the grant.

      Note what is NOT here: files.downloads, files.all, or any absolute-path
      access. If a recents menu is ever added, reopening a file across launches
      will need com.apple.security.files.bookmarks.app-scope plus real
      security-scoped bookmarks — a fresh path string alone will be denied.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      Required for the window to render at all, despite Paintlet making no
      network requests of any kind. WKWebView runs its networking in a separate
      XPC service, and under App Sandbox that service cannot initialise without
      this entitlement — even when every asset is local and served over Tauri's
      custom scheme. The web content process dies silently and the window opens
      completely blank.

      Verified, not assumed: a sandboxed build with only the two entitlements
      above renders nothing; adding this one and changing nothing else renders
      correctly. Confirmed in both directions on an existing container, so it is
      not a first-launch artefact.

      This PERMITS outbound connections; it does not create any. The app still
      has no network code. Word user-facing claims as "makes no network
      requests" rather than "has no network access", so the copy matches the
      entitlement a curious reviewer will read.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      Team-scoped identity. The prefix MUST be the Apple Team ID that owns the
      Mac App Store provisioning profile, and the suffix MUST equal the
      `identifier` in tauri.conf.json. Team R3557XH9FY is Yinan Ye's account —
      the same one docs/RELEASING.md signs Developer ID builds under.

      If you ship under a different Apple account, change the team ID in BOTH
      keys below. scripts/release-mas.sh cross-checks these against the signing
      identity and refuses to build on a mismatch, so a stale value fails loudly
      here rather than as an opaque rejection from App Store Connect.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.application-identifier</key>
    <string>R3557XH9FY.io.efficientnlp.paintlet</string>
    <key>com.apple.developer.team-identifier</key>
    <string>R3557XH9FY</string>
</dict>
</plist>
